---
.title = "The Cliffside",
.date = @date("2026-07-23T18:00:00+01:00"),
.author = "Garrett Moseke",
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = false,
.description = "A short story.",
---

Waves beat against the cliff face below like the low thrum of a sleeping heart. A man
perched quietly, his legs dangling absently over the edge, just high enough that the foam
barely kissed the bottom of his shoes. He sat, deep in thought, oblivious
to the wind and the spray and the grass and the birds drifting far above.

He was brought unceremoniously back to the present by the gentle press of footsteps approaching,
stopping just behind him. The man sighed to himself. He cleared his throat and said
without glancing backwards: "If you don't mind, I'd rather be left alone."

The man counted out a pair of heartbeats, then two deep breaths. The footsteps did not
recede, and the man's distraction quickly began to turn towards annoyance. The long silence
was only punctured when, rather than retreating, the footsteps took a seat beside him.
With a grumble, he glanced over to repeat himself, but stopped short as the stranger met
his gaze.

They were hard to describe--he could not tell if they were a man or a woman, and he was
sure he'd never seen them before. But their face was friendly, a look of impassive
curiosity set upon it. Neither spoke for a long minute, and just as the man began
to open his mouth to ask once more for privacy, the stranger simply asked, "Why?"

"What? What do you mean 'why?'", replied the man, furrowing his brow.

"Why do you want to be alone?"

The man gave a small shake of his head. Flustered for a response, he opened and closed his
mouth a few times, before finally shrugging and responding:

"I suppose there's no real reason to want to be alone. I was just thinking about a few
things and enjoying the quiet."

"Were you?" replied the stranger.

"I was. I have a lot of things on my mind," he replied tersely.

"That I don't doubt. I think you misunderstood my question. You said you were enjoying the
quiet. Were you?"

"Yes. I like it here, it's quiet, and calm, and a good place to think. Which I would like to
get back to, if that's alright with you."

The stranger simply nodded and remained silent for a long moment. Seeing that they made no
motion to rise, the man muttered softly to himself and began to get to his feet, resolving
to find somewhere else to sit. Just as he was standing up, however, the stranger spoke
again, with a hint of amusement in their voice:

"I suppose one could describe the sound of the waves and the wind and the birds as
peaceful. But I would not call it quiet. Calming perhaps. Or even beautiful. But not quiet."

The man thought to reply rather rudely, but as he did he hesitated. He supposed
the stranger was right, now that he thought about it for a moment.

The waves glanced off the rock below with a steady rhythm, soft, but certainly not quiet. The
wind ran atop the grass with a gentle, calming sigh, but it was by no means quiet. He had
meant it merely as a matter of semantics, of course, but as he began to say that to the
stranger he found he once again could not find the right words. The more he looked around
the cliffside and listened, the less and less quiet it seemed.

Intrigued, the man sat back down and looked upon his companion with a newfound curiosity.
They stared serenely back, meeting his gaze with a small smile. Neither said anything for
a long moment.

"I guess what I meant to say is that I needed somewhere without anyone else for a little
while. To organize my thoughts."

"Certainly." replied the stranger. "Everyone needs something like that from time to time.
But tell me, what sorts of thoughts did you need to organize?"

"What I was thinking about?" asked the man. He hesitated for a moment, tempted to say
that it was none of the stranger's business. But there was something about this person
that he couldn't explain. He didn't know exactly why, but he found himself taking a deep
breath and continuing.

"Well, I've been feeling this strange mix of apathy and loneliness that's hard to
explain. And I feel like I don't have any right to complain, because everything in my life
is so much easier than it was or even still is for lots of other people." He grunted in
dissatisfaction. "I'm not explaining it very well, I feel like I'm mostly regurgitating
things I've read other people say."

"We all do that from time to time. There isn't anything wrong with that." replied
the stranger after a long moment. "But tell me, why concern yourself with these things?"

The man thought for a long moment.

"Well... I guess I'm just wondering what the point of it is."

"The point of what?"

"It. Life. Being. Existence. Humanity, I guess. As a whole."

To the man's surprise, the stranger simply nodded, looking pensive.

"You wouldn't be the first to wonder, and I'm sure you know that you will be far from the
last."

The stranger gestured with their hand, brushing it lightly over the grass and letting a
few errant blades catch the salted breeze.

"This is a nice place to wonder about those sorts of things."

They turned to the man and smiled, warm and genuine and without a hint of pity or malice.
The man, to his own small surprise, smiled back, curious now about the person who sat beside him.

"You seem to have taken that in stride." he said, slightly surprised. "Lots of people
would throw a platitude back at me, or nod knowingly and agree, but you can tell that
they're only doing it because they don't know what to say. But you seem to just... listen.
How?"

The stranger gave a small chuckle.

"Well, I find myself talking to a lot of people, and sooner or later they
all end up thinking many of these same things. That's not to say that you're treading
churned ground, so to speak. Quite the contrary, I think it's one of the most important
things anyone can think about, really. Who you are. Why you're here. What matters and what
doesn't, and why that is."

They both fell silent again, eyes drawn to watch a bird drifting high above.

Just as the man was about to say something, the stranger pointed a long finger at it and spoke:

"What is the point of that falcon there?"

"What?" replied the man, confused. "I don't understand what you're asking. Well, I do, but
I don't see how it makes much sense. A falcon and a person aren't the same sort of thing."

"Why not?" said the stranger. "Both of you are alive. Both of you eat, and drink, and
breathe, and move, and so many other things."

"Yes," said the man, "but a falcon isn't aware that it's a falcon. Not in the way that I'm
aware that I'm a person. I think the scale of thinking about purpose is different in a way
that you can't really compare."

"Why not?" said the stranger again. "After all, there are a few billion others out there
that will do the same thing at one point or another in their lives. You said yourself that
you were echoing things other people have said. It's a different scale."

The man thought about this for a moment, before replying "I... suppose that's true. But
still, the falcon thinks of nothing more than its next meal. Its life is based on
instinct, not sapience."

"And that makes it different?"

"Well, yes. Not that its life means any less of course, but it can't imagine how
things could have been or may yet be. It can't conceptualize anything but the here and
the now. It can't be happy, or sad, or angry, or excited, or anything that we would call
human emotion. It can just react to the circumstances happening."

"And how do you know you are any different?" asked the stranger with amusement. "Who's to
say that all of that, those emotions and thinking about the future and the past, aren't
just instinct?"

The man opened his mouth to respond, but just as quickly closed it again. His brow
furrowed once more.

"...I suppose that's a fair point. But still. There's something about us, about people,
that's just, I don't know, it's *more*." He said this with conviction.

The stranger simply nodded. "I agree with you, of course," they said. "But just because
it's more doesn't make it any different."

The man could think of nothing to say, and they sat there for a few minutes in
companionable silence as a cloud passed above. Finally, the man broached the conversation
again.

"I guess I've just always felt that things would be different. Should be different."

"What do you mean?" replied the stranger.

"I guess I've just always had this picture in my head" continued the man. "It's nothing
very special. It's just me, sitting in a chair next to a bed and reading one of my
favorite books to my daughter while she drifts to sleep. I always imagine it as my daughter.
I don't know why. She's maybe five or six? And she falls asleep and I close the book and I
smile. And in that picture I feel happy. And I feel like if that picture comes true, then
my life will have meant something, at the least. But I'm terrified that it won't." The man
sighed.

"That it won't happen, or that it won't have meant something?" asked the stranger calmly.

"Both. Either I suppose. Mostly the first one, but there's certainly bits of the second.
And there are so many things like that. Things I spend my time on that I regret as soon as
they're finished. Things I tell myself I need to do, and then don't. Occasional times
where I feel like everything is working and I'm finally back on track, but then inevitably
something happens and I lose the track again. And I know it's not supposed to be a clear
path all the way through, I know that. People far more eloquent than me have brought up
that aspect of being human. It just feels like... I don't know. It feels like I should be
able to look back at the billions and billions before me, and know what I'm supposed to
*do*. Or at least have some sort of idea." The man shook his head, out of words to say.

The stranger simply sat for a moment, looking out over the waves, watching as the falcon
circled lazily above. The breeze picked up again and blew through the grass once more, a
soft whisper that surrounded them.

Finally, the stranger spoke.

"I don't know the answer either," they said. "And if anyone should, it should be me. But I
accepted long, long ago that I *can't* know the answer. The only person that knows the
answer to the questions you're asking is you, just as everyone that's come before you. And
just like they did, you won't know the answer until we talk again."

"What do you mean?" Asked the man, confused.

Death rose quietly to their feet. "We will see each other again. It may be
tomorrow, or it may be many years. But when we meet once more I will embrace you with a
smile, knowing that you are finally ready to finish our conversation."

